(window.RW_DISPLAY = window.RW_DISPLAY || {})["test08_2A"] = [{"id":"ed52a093","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill":"Cross-Text Connections","difficulty":"Easy","context":"Craft and Structure · Cross-Text Connections","passage":"Text 1 Flamingos are known for their vibrant pink coloring, but they’re actually born with gray feathers. Their pink color comes from eating brine shrimp, but brine shrimp aren’t naturally pink either. Animals can’t produce carotenoids, the pigments that provide the pink hue. The algae that brine shrimp feed on, however, can produce these pigments. Thus, the pinker the flamingo, the more shrimp it has eaten. Text 2 Ecologist Juan Amat has found that flamingos apply a kind of makeup to make themselves appear pinker. A gland near their tail contains pigments that come from the food they eat. When the flamingos groom themselves using the pigments, their feathers become pinker. Flamingos may do this to improve their success during mating season, when they would benefit from looking pinker.","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"Text 1","indent":0},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"line","text":"Flamingos are known for their vibrant pink coloring, but they’re actually born with gray feathers. Their pink color comes from eating brine shrimp,","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"but brine shrimp aren’t naturally pink either. Animals can’t produce carotenoids, the pigments that provide the pink hue. The algae that brine","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"shrimp feed on, however, can produce these pigments. Thus, the pinker the flamingo, the more shrimp it has eaten.","indent":0},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"line","text":"Text 2","indent":0},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"line","text":"Ecologist Juan Amat has found that flamingos apply a kind of makeup to make themselves appear pinker. A gland near their tail contains","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"pigments that come from the food they eat. When the flamingos groom themselves using the pigments, their feathers become pinker. Flamingos","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"may do this to improve their success during mating season, when they would benefit from looking pinker.","indent":0}],"question":"Based on the texts, how would the ecologist in Text 2 most likely respond to the author’s conclusion in Text 1?","choices":["By emphasizing that flamingos’ tail feathers are pinker than their other feathers are","By claiming that the coloring of flamingos’ feathers doesn’t change significantly enough for most observers to notice","By pointing out that the amount of shrimp eaten isn’t the only thing that influences flamingos’ coloring","By arguing that flamingos’ diet doesn’t include much shrimp except during mating season"]},{"id":"5d48f889","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill":"Words in Context","difficulty":"Easy","context":"Craft and Structure · Words in Context","passage":"The following text is adapted from Mercedes de Acosta’s 1921 poem “Spring and You.” Now, Love and April, and the gold of your hair, Are all mingled together Like the blending of an exotic dream plant With the fragrant perfume of a strange, frail flower. As used in the text,","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"The following text is adapted from Mercedes de Acosta’s 1921 poem “Spring and You.”","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"Now, Love and April, and the gold of your","indent":2},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"line","text":"hair,","indent":3},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"line","text":"Are all mingled together","indent":2},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"line","text":"Like the blending of an exotic dream plant","indent":2},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"line","text":"With the fragrant perfume of a strange, frail","indent":2},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"line","text":"flower.","indent":3}],"question":"As used in the text, what does the word “mingled” most nearly mean?","choices":["Identified","Combined","Remembered","Celebrated"],"underline":"mingled","underline_segments":["mingled"]},{"id":"9cdcd902","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill":"Words in Context","difficulty":"Easy","context":"Craft and Structure · Words in Context","passage":"Charles “Teenie” Harris was a photographer for the Pittsburgh Courier from 1936 to 1975. During his career he took over 70,000 photographs documenting everyday life in Pittsburgh’s Black communities. The Carnegie Museum of Art maintains thousands of his photographs, carefully ______ them so that audiences can continue to view them well into the future.","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"Charles “Teenie” Harris was a photographer for the Pittsburgh Courier from 1936 to 1975. During his career he took over 70,000 photographs","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"documenting everyday life in Pittsburgh’s Black communities. The Carnegie Museum of Art maintains thousands of his photographs, carefully","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"______ them so that audiences can continue to view them well into the future.","indent":0}],"question":"Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?","choices":["replacing","inventing","preserving","counting"]},{"id":"3d658a5a","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill":"Words in Context","difficulty":"Hard","context":"Craft and Structure · Words in Context","passage":"Some foraging models predict that the distance bees travel when foraging will decline as floral density increases, but biologists Shalene Jha and Claire Kremen showed that bees behavior is inconsistent with this prediction if flowers in dense patches are ______: bees will forage beyond patches of low species richness to acquire multiple resource types.","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"Some foraging models predict that the distance bees travel when foraging will decline as floral density increases, but biologists Shalene Jha and","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"Claire Kremen showed that bees’ behavior is inconsistent with this prediction if flowers in dense patches are ______: bees will forage beyond","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"patches of low species richness to acquire multiple resource types.","indent":0}],"question":"Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?","choices":["depleted","homogeneous","immature","dispersed"]},{"id":"85083d4d","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill":"Words in Context","difficulty":"Medium","context":"Craft and Structure · Words in Context","passage":"Disproving the common misconception of Native art as ______, the painters whose work appears in the collection at the National Museum of the American Indian employ a range of styles. There are artists working in the traditional arts of their specific tribal communities, artists working in European modernist or American abstract expressionist art traditions, and artists blending various traditions into something wholly new.","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"Disproving the common misconception of Native art as ______, the painters whose work appears in the collection at the National Museum of the","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"American Indian employ a range of styles. There are artists working in the traditional arts of their specific tribal communities, artists working in","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"European modernist or American abstract expressionist art traditions, and artists blending various traditions into something wholly new.","indent":0}],"question":"Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?","choices":["uncontroversial","individualistic","theoretical","homogeneous"]},{"id":"835d1ae6","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill":"Cross-Text Connections","difficulty":"Easy","context":"Craft and Structure · Cross-Text Connections","passage":"Text 1 Historians studying pre-Inca Peru have looked to ceramic vessels to understand daily life among the Moche people. These mold-made sculptures present plants, animals, and human faces in precise ways — vessels representing human faces are so detailed that scholars have interpreted facial markings to represent scars and other skin irregularities. Some historians have even used these objects to identify potential skin diseases that may have afflicted people at the time. Text 2 Art historian and archaeologist Lisa Trever has argued that the interpretation of Moche “portrait” vessels as hyper-realistic portrayals of identifiable people may inadvertently disregard the creativity of the objects creators. Moche ceramic vessels, Trever argues, are artworks in which sculptors could free their imagination, using realistic objects and people around them as inspiration to explore more abstract concepts. Based on the texts,","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"Text 1","indent":0},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"line","text":"Historians studying pre-Inca Peru have looked to ceramic vessels to understand daily life among the Moche people. These mold-made sculptures","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"present plants, animals, and human faces in precise ways—vessels representing human faces are so detailed that scholars have interpreted","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"facial markings to represent scars and other skin irregularities. Some historians have even used these objects to identify potential skin diseases","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"that may have afflicted people at the time.","indent":0},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"line","text":"Text 2","indent":0},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"line","text":"Art historian and archaeologist Lisa Trever has argued that the interpretation of Moche “portrait” vessels as hyper-realistic portrayals of","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"identifiable people may inadvertently disregard the creativity of the objects’ creators. Moche ceramic vessels, Trever argues, are artworks in","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"which sculptors could free their imagination, using realistic objects and people around them as inspiration to explore more abstract concepts.","indent":0}],"question":"Based on the texts, what would Lisa Trever (Text 2) most likely say about the interpretation presented in the underlined portion of Text 1?","choices":["Depictions of human faces are significantly more realistic than depictions of plants and other animals are.","It is likely that some depictions of human faces with extensive markings are intended to portray the same historical individual.","Some vessels may have been damaged during their excavation and thus provide little insight into Moche culture.","Markings on depictions of human faces are not necessarily intended to portray particular details about the physical appearance of individuals."],"underline":null,"underline_segments":["scholars have interpreted","facial markings to represent scars and other skin irregularities."]},{"id":"a20b7511","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill":"Words in Context","difficulty":"Easy","context":"Craft and Structure · Words in Context","passage":"Scholars long thought that the initial spread of silk beyond China occurred in the second century CE, but this view has been ______ by new archaeological evidence from South Asia that reveals that the people of the Indus Civilization made use of silk at least 1,000 years earlier.","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"Scholars long thought that the initial spread of silk beyond China occurred in the second century CE, but this view has been ______ by new","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"archaeological evidence from South Asia that reveals that the people of the Indus Civilization made use of silk at least 1,000 years earlier.","indent":0}],"question":"Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?","choices":["investigated","misinterpreted","anticipated","contradicted"]},{"id":"7bc05fa2","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill":"Words in Context","difficulty":"Hard","context":"Craft and Structure · Words in Context","passage":"Whether the reign of a French monarch such as Hugh Capet or Henry I was historically consequential or relatively uneventful, its trajectory was shaped by questions of legitimacy and therefore cannot be understood without a corollary understanding of the factors that allowed the monarch to ______ his right to hold the throne.","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"Whether the reign of a French monarch such as Hugh Capet or Henry I was historically consequential or relatively uneventful, its trajectory was","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"shaped by questions of legitimacy and therefore cannot be understood without a corollary understanding of the factors that allowed the","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"monarch to ______ his right to hold the throne.","indent":0}],"question":"Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?","choices":["disengage","annotate","buttress","reciprocate"]},{"id":"224428ac","domain":"Information and Ideas","skill":"Command of Evidence","difficulty":"Medium","context":"Information and Ideas · Command of Evidence","passage":"California Condor Populations 2014–2020 400 Number of condors 300 200 100 0 14 15 16 17 18 19 20 20 20 20 20 20 20 20 Year captive wild The California Condor Recovery Program is working to protect this large, rare American vulture from extinction. The program captured 27 wild condors in 1987 in order to protect and breed them. Captive birds have been reintroduced into the wild since 1992. Although the California condor is still a rare species, many biologists claim that the program has been reasonably successful.","passage_lines":[{"type":"chart","note":"[Graph: California Condor Populations, 2014–2020 — not available here]"},{"type":"text","text":"The California Condor Recovery Program is working to protect this large, rare American vulture from extinction. The program captured 27 wild condors in 1987 in order to protect and breed them. Captive birds have been reintroduced into the wild since 1992. Although the California condor is still a rare species, many biologists claim that the program has been reasonably successful."}],"question":"Which choice best describes data from the graph that support the biologists’ claim?","choices":["The number of captive California condors steadily decreased between 2014 and 2020.","The numbers of California condors living both in captivity and in the wild decreased only slightly from 2019 to 2020.","The difference between the number of captive and wild California condors remained relatively steady between 2014 and 2017.","The number of wild California condors increased overall from 2014 to 2020 and exceeded the number of those living in captivity."]},{"id":"ed314256","domain":"Information and Ideas","skill":"Central Ideas and Details","difficulty":"Hard","context":"Information and Ideas · Central Ideas and Details","passage":"The most recent iteration of the immersive theater experience Sleep No More, which premiered in New York City in 2011, transforms its performance space — a five-story warehouse— into a 1930s-era hotel. Audience members, who wander through the labyrinthine venue at their own pace and follow the actors as they play out simultaneous, interweaving narrative loops, confront the impossibility of experiencing the production in its entirety. The play’s refusal of narrative coherence thus hinges on the sense of spatial fragmentation that the venue’s immense and intricate layout generates.","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"The most recent iteration of the immersive theater experience Sleep No More, which premiered in New York City in 2011, transforms its","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"performance space—a five-story warehouse—into a 1930s-era hotel. Audience members, who wander through the labyrinthine venue at their own","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"pace and follow the actors as they play out simultaneous, interweaving narrative loops, confront the impossibility of experiencing the production","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"in its entirety. The play’s refusal of narrative coherence thus hinges on the sense of spatial fragmentation that the venue’s immense and intricate","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"layout generates.","indent":0}],"question":"What does the text most strongly suggest about Sleep No More’s use of its performance space?","choices":["The choice of a New York City venue likely enabled the play’s creators to experiment with the use of theatrical space in a way that venues from earlier productions could not.","Audience members likely find the experience of the play disappointing because they generally cannot make their way through the entire venue.","The production’s dependence on a particular performance environment would likely make it difficult to reproduce exactly in a different theatrical space.","Audience members who navigate the space according to a recommended itinerary will likely have a better grasp of the play’s narrative than audience members who depart from that itinerary."]},{"id":"350e2336","domain":"Information and Ideas","skill":"Inferences","difficulty":"Medium","context":"Information and Ideas · Inferences","passage":"The Haitian Declaration of Independence was issued in 1804, bringing to an end the revolution against colonial France that began in 1791. Written in French, which was not the first language of most Haitians but which was used throughout Europe as the language of international diplomacy, the declaration notes that Haiti will not bring rebellion to other Caribbean nations, promises to respect the sovereignty of its neighbors — widely understood as a reassurance to the United States —and sets up Haiti as an example for future struggles against colonizers (an implicit reference to the many colonies then found in the Americas). So even though the declaration is explicitly addressed to the Haitian people, it’s reasonable to conclude that ______","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"The Haitian Declaration of Independence was issued in 1804, bringing to an end the revolution against colonial France that began in 1791.","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"Written in French, which was not the first language of most Haitians but which was used throughout Europe as the language of international","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"diplomacy, the declaration notes that Haiti will not bring rebellion to other Caribbean nations, promises to respect the sovereignty of its","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"neighbors—widely understood as a reassurance to the United States—and sets up Haiti as an example for future struggles against colonizers (an","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"implicit reference to the many colonies then found in the Americas). So even though the declaration is explicitly addressed to the Haitian people,","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"it’s reasonable to conclude that ______","indent":0}],"question":"Which choice most logically completes the text?","choices":["aspects of the declaration were modeled on similar documents from other countries.","the French government may have been surprised by the declaration.","many Haitian people opposed the revolution and the declaration.","the declaration actually had several intended audiences."]},{"id":"ca5a3fb4","domain":"Information and Ideas","skill":"Inferences","difficulty":"Medium","context":"Information and Ideas · Inferences","passage":"The practice of logging (cutting down trees for commercial and other uses) is often thought to be at odds with forest conservation (the work of preserving forests). However, a massive study in forest management and preservation spanning 700,000 hectares in Oregon’s Malheur National Forest calls that view into question. So far, results of the study suggest that forest plots that have undergone limited logging (the careful removal of a controlled number of trees) may be more robust than plots that haven’t been logged at all. These results, in turn, suggest that ______","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"The practice of logging (cutting down trees for commercial and other uses) is often thought to be at odds with forest conservation (the work of","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"preserving forests). However, a massive study in forest management and preservation spanning 700,000 hectares in Oregon’s Malheur National","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"Forest calls that view into question. So far, results of the study suggest that forest plots that have undergone limited logging (the careful removal","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"of a controlled number of trees) may be more robust than plots that haven’t been logged at all. These results, in turn, suggest that ______","indent":0}],"question":"Which choice most logically completes the text?","choices":["logging may be useful for maintaining healthy forests, provided it is limited.","other forest management strategies are more effective than limited logging.","as time passes, it will be difficult to know whether limited logging has any benefits.","the best way to support forest health may be to leave large forests entirely untouched."]},{"id":"3ae2638c","domain":"Information and Ideas","skill":"Inferences","difficulty":"Medium","context":"Information and Ideas · Inferences","passage":"In documents called judicial opinions, judges explain the reasoning behind their legal rulings, and in those explanations they sometimes cite and discuss historical and contemporary philosophers. Legal scholar and philosopher Anita L. Allen argues that while judges are naturally inclined to mention philosophers whose views align with their own positions, the strongest judicial opinions consider and rebut potential objections; discussing philosophers whose views conflict with judges’ views could therefore ______","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"In documents called judicial opinions, judges explain the reasoning behind their legal rulings, and in those explanations they sometimes cite and","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"discuss historical and contemporary philosophers. Legal scholar and philosopher Anita L. Allen argues that while judges are naturally inclined to","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"mention philosophers whose views align with their own positions, the strongest judicial opinions consider and rebut potential objections;","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"discussing philosophers whose views conflict with judges’ views could therefore ______","indent":0}],"question":"Which choice most logically completes the text?","choices":["allow judges to craft judicial opinions without needing to consult philosophical works.","help judges improve the arguments they put forward in their judicial opinions.","make judicial opinions more comprehensible to readers without legal or philosophical training.","bring judicial opinions in line with views that are broadly held among philosophers."]},{"id":"40630cef","domain":"Information and Ideas","skill":"Central Ideas and Details","difficulty":"Easy","context":"Information and Ideas · Central Ideas and Details","passage":"The following text is from David Barclay Moore’s 2022 novel Holler of the Fireflies. The narrator has just arrived at summer camp, which is far away from his home. This place was different than I thought it would be. I’d never been somewhere like this before. I did feel scared, but also excited. ©2022 by David Barclay Moore","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"The following text is from David Barclay Moore’s 2022 novel Holler of the Fireflies. The narrator has just arrived at summer camp, which is far","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"away from his home.","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"This place was different than I thought it would be. I’d never been somewhere like this before. I did feel scared, but also excited.","indent":2},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"line","text":"©2022 by David Barclay Moore","indent":4}],"question":"According to the text, how does the narrator feel about being at summer camp?","choices":["He feels overjoyed.","He feels peaceful.","He feels both scared and excited.","He feels both angry and jealous."]},{"id":"4411e15b","domain":"Information and Ideas","skill":"Command of Evidence","difficulty":"Hard","context":"Information and Ideas · Command of Evidence","passage":"Fiber Characteristics of Mouflon, Navajo-Churro, and Spanish Merino Sheep Type of sheep Diameter of outer coat fibers (in microns) Diameter of inner coat fibers (in microns) Spanish Merino 19 – 24 17 –21 Navajo-Churro 35 or higher 10 –35 Mouflon 150 15 Domestic sheep’s wild ancestor, the mouflon, has a coarse outer coat and an inner coat of wool fiber that is finer in diameter and therefore much softer. In some domestic breeds, such as the Spanish Merino, the outer fiber is only marginally coarser than the inner, and the wool is soft overall. Thus, Merino wool is ideal for delicate garments worn against the skin. Meanwhile, the Navajo-Churro has been selected to retain the marked distinction between outer and inner fiber that the Merino has lost. Being coarser than Merino wool overall, Navajo-Churro wool yields a more durable yarn, which Diné (Navajo) weavers use in their celebrated rugs. Yet a comparison of the fiber characteristics of all three sheep reveals that ______","passage_lines":[{"type":"table","title":"Fiber Characteristics of Mouflon, Navajo-Churro, and Spanish Merino Sheep","headers":["Type of sheep","Diameter of outer coat fibers (in microns)","Diameter of inner coat fibers (in microns)"],"rows":[["Spanish Merino","19–24","17–21"],["Navajo-Churro","35 or higher","10–35"],["Mouflon","150","15"]]},{"type":"text","text":"Domestic sheep’s wild ancestor, the mouflon, has a coarse outer coat and an inner coat of wool fiber that is finer in diameter and therefore much softer. In some domestic breeds, such as the Spanish Merino, the outer fiber is only marginally coarser than the inner, and the wool is soft overall. Thus, Merino wool is ideal for delicate garments worn against the skin. Meanwhile, the Navajo-Churro has been selected to retain the marked distinction between outer and inner fiber that the Merino has lost. Being coarser than Merino wool overall, Navajo-Churro wool yields a more durable yarn, which Diné (Navajo) weavers use in their celebrated rugs. Yet a comparison of the fiber characteristics of all three sheep reveals that ______"}],"question":"Which choice most effectively uses data from the table to complete the text?","choices":["the selection process that enabled the Navajo-Churro to retain its somewhat coarse outer fiber also resulted in inner fiber that, at its softest, is softer than either the mouflon’s or the Merino’s inner fiber.","the Navajo-Churro more closely resembles its ancestor, the mouflon, in the uniform softness of its inner fiber, while the Merino more closely resembles the mouflon in the variable diameter of its outer fiber.","domestication resulted in a counterintuitive increase in the inner fiber’s minimum diameter, making the inner fiber of the Merino and the Navajo-Churro less suitable for delicate garments than the mouflon’s inner fiber is.","the domestication of the mouflon and the subsequent selection process that produced the Merino and the Navajo-Churro resulted in greater softness of outer and inner fiber alike."],"underline":null,"underline_segments":["Fiber","Fiber Characteristics of Mouflon, Navajo-Churro,","and Spanish Merino Sheep"]},{"id":"bb4557cf","domain":"Standard English","skill":"Boundaries","difficulty":"Hard","context":"Standard English · Boundaries","passage":"The relationship between genomes and epigenomes reveals how cells with identical DNA develop different ______ whereas the genome in each cell contains a complete DNA sequence, the epigenome consists of chemical compounds that determine which traits in the sequence will be expressed.","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"The relationship between genomes and epigenomes reveals how cells with identical DNA develop different ______ whereas the genome in each","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"cell contains a complete DNA sequence, the epigenome consists of chemical compounds that determine which traits in the sequence will be","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"expressed.","indent":0}],"question":"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","choices":["functions","functions,","functions and,","functions:"]},{"id":"b6560e5a","domain":"Standard English","skill":"Boundaries","difficulty":"Medium","context":"Standard English · Boundaries","passage":"Materials scientist Marie-Agathe Charpagne and her colleagues believed they could improve on the multicomponent alloy NiCoCr, an equal- proportions mixture of nickel (Ni), cobalt (Co), and chromium (Cr), by replacing chromium with ruthenium ______ the alloy that resulted, NiCoRu, turned out to be an unsuitable replacement for NiCoCr.","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"Materials scientist Marie-Agathe Charpagne and her colleagues believed they could improve on the multicomponent alloy NiCoCr, an equal-","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"proportions mixture of nickel (Ni), cobalt (Co), and chromium (Cr), by replacing chromium with ruthenium ______ the alloy that resulted, NiCoRu,","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"turned out to be an unsuitable replacement for NiCoCr.","indent":0}],"question":"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","choices":["(Ru)","(Ru) but","(Ru),","(Ru), but"]},{"id":"d2cf0e11","domain":"Standard English","skill":"Form, Structure, and Sense","difficulty":"Medium","context":"Standard English · Form, Structure, and Sense","passage":"Inventor John Friedman created a prototype of the first flexible straw by inserting a screw into a paper straw and, using dental floss, binding the straw tightly around the ______ When the floss and screw were removed, the resulting corrugations in the paper allowed the straw to bend easily over the edge of a glass.","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"Inventor John Friedman created a prototype of the first flexible straw by inserting a screw into a paper straw and, using dental floss, binding the","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"straw tightly around the ______ When the floss and screw were removed, the resulting corrugations in the paper allowed the straw to bend easily","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"over the edge of a glass.","indent":0}],"question":"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","choices":["screw’s thread’s.","screws’ threads.","screw’s threads.","screws threads’."]},{"id":"8a264a54","domain":"Standard English","skill":"Boundaries","difficulty":"Hard","context":"Standard English · Boundaries","passage":"With a blend of traditional design elements, such as arched Gothic ceilings, and modern ones, such as floor-to-ceiling ______ design splits the difference between old and new, a mixture that is increasingly seen in home interiors in the US.","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"With a blend of traditional design elements, such as arched Gothic ceilings, and modern ones, such as floor-to-ceiling ______ design splits the","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"difference between old and new, a mixture that is increasingly seen in home interiors in the US.","indent":0}],"question":"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","choices":["windows; transitional","windows — transitional","windows. Transitional","windows, transitional"]},{"id":"8790d061","domain":"Standard English","skill":"Boundaries","difficulty":"Medium","context":"Standard English · Boundaries","passage":"While constructing the Limyra Bridge, ancient Roman builders relied on temporary wooden beams to support the stone and brick structure. These temporary supports, a construction system known as ______ were removed when the bridge was completed.","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"While constructing the Limyra Bridge, ancient Roman builders relied on temporary wooden beams to support the stone and brick structure.","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"These temporary supports, a construction system known as ______ were removed when the bridge was completed.","indent":0}],"question":"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","choices":["falsework;","falsework","falsework,","falsework:"]},{"id":"512f0ac9","domain":"Standard English","skill":"Form, Structure, and Sense","difficulty":"Hard","context":"Standard English · Form, Structure, and Sense","passage":"Working from an earlier discovery of Charpentier’s, chemists Emmanuelle Charpentier and Jennifer Doudna — winners of the 2020 Nobel Prize in Chemistry —re-created and then reprogrammed the so-called “genetic scissors” of a species of DNA-cleaving bacteria ______ a tool that is revolutionizing the field of gene technology.","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"Working from an earlier discovery of Charpentier’s, chemists Emmanuelle Charpentier and Jennifer Doudna—winners of the 2020 Nobel Prize in","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"Chemistry—re-created and then reprogrammed the so-called “genetic scissors” of a species of DNA-cleaving bacteria ______ a tool that is","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"revolutionizing the field of gene technology.","indent":0}],"question":"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","choices":["to forge","forging","forged","and forging"]},{"id":"176edca6","domain":"Expression of Ideas","skill":"Transitions","difficulty":"Hard","context":"Expression of Ideas · Transitions","passage":"A 2017 study of sign language learners tested the role of iconicity — — the similarity of a sign to the thing it represents in language acquisition. The study found that the greater the iconicity of a sign, the more likely it was to have been learned. ______ the correlation between acquisition and iconicity was lower than that between acquisition and another factor studied: sign frequency.","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"A 2017 study of sign language learners tested the role of iconicity—the similarity of a sign to the thing it represents—in language acquisition. The","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"study found that thegreater the iconicity of a sign, the more likely it was to have been learned. ______ the correlation between acquisition and","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"iconicity was lower than that between acquisition and another factor studied: sign frequency.","indent":0}],"question":"Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?","choices":["In fact,","In other words,","Granted,","As a result,"]},{"id":"4154a7a3","domain":"Expression of Ideas","skill":"Transitions","difficulty":"Easy","context":"Expression of Ideas · Transitions","passage":"In 1891, dancer and choreographer Loie Fuller first performed her celebrated Serpentine Dance, artfully twirling her long, flowing skirt to create striking visual effects. ______ in 1896, cinema pioneers Auguste and Louis Lumière made a groundbreaking short film of Fuller’s dance.","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"In 1891, dancer and choreographer Loie Fuller first performed her celebrated Serpentine Dance, artfully twirling her long, flowing skirt to create","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"striking visual effects. ______ in 1896, cinema pioneers Auguste and Louis Lumière made a groundbreaking short film of Fuller’s dance.","indent":0}],"question":"Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?","choices":["However,","In conclusion,","Later,","In other words,"]},{"id":"6249b173","domain":"Expression of Ideas","skill":"Rhetorical Synthesis","difficulty":"Medium","context":"Expression of Ideas · Rhetorical Synthesis","passage":"While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes: In 2018 researchers Adwait Deshpande, Shreejata Gupta, and Anindya Sinha were observing wild macaques in India’s Bandipur National Park. They saw macaques calling out to and gesturing at humans who were eating or carrying food. They designed a study to find out if the macaques were intentionally communicating to try to persuade the humans to share their food. In the study trials, macaques frequently called out to and gestured at humans holding food. In the study trials, macaques called out to and gestured at empty-handed humans less frequently. The student wants to present the study’s results.","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"In 2018 researchers Adwait Deshpande, Shreejata Gupta, and Anindya Sinha were observing wild macaques in India’s Bandipur National","indent":2},{"type":"line","text":"Park.","indent":2},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"line","text":"They saw macaques calling out to and gesturing at humans who were eating or carrying food.","indent":2},{"type":"line","text":"They designed a study to find out if the macaques were intentionally communicating to try to persuade the humans to share their food.","indent":2},{"type":"line","text":"In the study trials, macaques frequently called out to and gestured at humans holding food.","indent":2},{"type":"line","text":"In the study trials, macaques called out to and gestured at empty-handed humans less frequently.","indent":2}],"question":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Macaques in the study called out to and gestured more frequently at humans holding food than at empty-handed humans.","In 2018, researchers who had observed macaques in India’s Bandipur National Park calling out to and gesturing at humans designed a study.","The researchers hoped to find out if the macaques were intentionally communicating to try to persuade humans to share their food.","The researchers studied how macaques behaved around both humans holding food and empty-handed humans."]},{"id":"129089b5","domain":"Expression of Ideas","skill":"Transitions","difficulty":"Medium","context":"Expression of Ideas · Transitions","passage":"In 1933, the Twentieth Amendment to the US Constitution was ratified. The amendment mandates that presidential inaugurations be held on January 20, approximately ten weeks after the November election. ______ this amendment requires newly elected US senators and representatives to be sworn into their respective offices on January 3.","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"In 1933, the Twentieth Amendment to the US Constitution was ratified. The amendment mandates that presidential inaugurations be held on","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"January 20, approximately ten weeks after the November election. ______ this amendment requires newly elected US senators and","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"representatives to be sworn into their respective offices on January 3.","indent":0}],"question":"Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?","choices":["Instead,","For instance,","Specifically,","In addition,"]},{"id":"911c7a87","domain":"Expression of Ideas","skill":"Transitions","difficulty":"Easy","context":"Expression of Ideas · Transitions","passage":"After appropriate permissions are granted, a typical archaeological dig begins with a surveyor making a detailed grid of the excavation site. Then, the site is carefully dug, and any artifacts found are recorded and mapped onto the site grid. ______ the artifacts are removed, cataloged, and analyzed in a laboratory.","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"After appropriate permissions are granted, a typical archaeological dig begins with a surveyor making a detailed grid of the excavation site. Then,","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"the site is carefully dug, and any artifacts found are recorded and mapped onto the site grid. ______ the artifacts are removed, cataloged, and","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"analyzed in a laboratory.","indent":0}],"question":"Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?","choices":["For instance,","On the contrary,","Earlier,","Finally,"]},{"id":"d6dec50e","domain":"Expression of Ideas","skill":"Rhetorical Synthesis","difficulty":"Medium","context":"Expression of Ideas · Rhetorical Synthesis","passage":"While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes: In 2019, Emily Shepard and colleagues in the UK and Germany studied the effect of wind on auks success in landing at cliffside nesting sites. They found as wind conditions intensified, the birds needed more attempts in order to make a successful landing. When the wind was still, almost 100% of landing attempts were successful. In a strong breeze, approximately 40% of attempts were successful. In near-gale conditions, only around 20% of attempts were successful. The student wants to summarize the study.","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"In 2019, Emily Shepard and colleagues in the UK and Germany studied the effect of wind on auks’ success in landing at cliffside nesting","indent":2},{"type":"line","text":"sites.","indent":2},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"line","text":"They found as wind conditions intensified, the birds needed more attempts in order to make a successful landing.","indent":2},{"type":"line","text":"When the wind was still, almost 100% of landing attempts were successful.","indent":2},{"type":"line","text":"In a strong breeze, approximately 40% of attempts were successful.","indent":2},{"type":"line","text":"In near-gale conditions, only around 20% of attempts were successful.","indent":2}],"question":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["For a 2019 study, researchers from the UK and Germany collected data on auks attempts to land at cliffside nesting sites in different wind conditions.","Emily Shepard and her colleagues wanted to know the extent to which wind affected auks success in landing at cliffside nesting sites, so they conducted a study.","Knowing that auks often need multiple attempts to land at their cliffside nesting sites, Emily Shepard studied the birds success rate, which was only around 20% in some conditions.","Emily Shepard’s 2019 study of auks success in landing at cliffside nesting sites showed that as wind conditions intensified, the birds success rate decreased."]}];
